# GC build agreement: explicit, not luck

Review findings 9 + 10. ODR safety currently rests on __has_include
agreeing across TUs; the no-GC fallback branch is unreachable dead
code in this build graph (c_eval.pxd includes gc/gc.h
unconditionally). Also: EvalState.force and Value accessors skip
gc_register_current_thread - safe only transitively today, unsafe when
forcing a value produced by another state's runner.

Fix: pass -DFAKE_LIBRARY_USE_BOEHMGC=1 explicitly from bindings
setup.py (or #error on disagreement); register in force + Value
accessors; delete or consciously keep the fallback branch.

## Done 2026-08-25

FAKE_LIBRARY_USE_BOEHMGC is now mandatory. gc-env.hpp `#error`s when
it is undefined, meson.build and setup.py both pass it, and bdw-gc
became a required meson dependency. __has_include detection is gone:
it made ODR agreement depend on every TU happening to see the same
include path, which held by luck and would have broken silently.

The no-GC fallback branch is DELETED rather than kept. It could never
compile in this build graph - c_eval.pxd includes gc/gc.h and binds
GC_malloc_uncollectable directly - so keeping it preserved the
appearance of a tested path that had never been built. Real Nix does
support a no-GC build; restoring one here means building and testing
it.

Registration: added to EvalState.force, and to Value.__dealloc__ -
which the task did not name and which was the worse of the two.
__dealloc__ runs on whichever thread drops the last Python reference,
rarely the producing runner: the asyncio loop, a pool worker, the
server's handle reaper. GC_free takes the collector lock and may have
to cooperate with a collection.

DELIBERATELY NOT added to the Value accessors. Reading a value needs
no registration: the uncollectable bridge cell keeps it reachable from
any thread, so an unscanned stack cannot lose it. The header says so,
since the asymmetry reads as an oversight otherwise. Revisit if an
accessor ever allocates.
